<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amalie Elisabeth of Hanau-Münzenberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1602">1602</a>–<a href="/years/1651">1651</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Amalie Elisabeth</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amalie Elisabeth of Hanau-Münzenberg was the wife of Landgrave William V of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> and regent for her son William VI from 1637 to 1650. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> she kept Hesse-Kassel in alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, held its army together and won the Hessian War against Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> she secured territorial gains and the recognition of the Reformed faith.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amalie-elisabeth-of-hanau-munzenberg.jpg" alt="Amalie Elisabeth of Hanau-Münzenberg" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerard van Honthorst · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amalie_Elisabeth_von_Hanau-M%C3%BCnzenberg_(1602%E2%80%931651).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Amalie Elisabeth was born on 28 January 1602 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-louis-ii-count-of-hanau-munzenberg">Philip Louis II, Count of Hanau-Münzenberg</a>, and Countess Catharina Belgica of Nassau, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a>. She lived for a time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> with her aunt Countess Louise Juliana of Nassau and Frederick IV, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a>, and later with relatives in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Her first betrothed, the Bohemian noble Albrecht Jan Smiřický, died in 1618. She married William of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> in 1619, and of their 14 children only four lived to adulthood. When William became landgrave in 1627 she ran affairs at home while he was on campaign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1637 Imperial victories drove the family into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a>, where William V died at Leer on 1 October, leaving her regent for their son William VI and his army as her chief asset. She sidelined the regency council, kept command with General Peter Melander until his resignation in 1640, and refused the harsh terms of Landgrave George II of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>. By the Treaty of Dorsten of August 1639 she renewed the French alliance with a larger subsidy than her husband had received, and Hessian troops fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campine">Kempen</a> and the Second Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-breitenfeld-1631">Breitenfeld</a>.</p><p>In 1645 she invaded and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a>, opening the Hessian War over the Marburg inheritance her father-in-law Maurice had lost. Melander, now in Imperial service, failed to take the city's fortress, and an agreement of April 1648 gave her most of the disputed land. At the Westphalian congress she also gained Hersfeld Abbey, parts of the County of Schaumburg and 600,000 Reichsthalers, and she was the only German ruler to secure a payment to demobilise her army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>She abdicated at a ceremony on 5 October 1650, after William VI had turned 21. On a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> in 1651, where the inhabitants hailed her as a second Deborah, part of her foot had to be amputated, and she died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> in August that year. She was one of four women admitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walhalla">Walhalla</a> in the lifetime of King Ludwig I, with a bust by Christian Friedrich Tieck. Tryntje Helfferich, author of The Iron Princess (2013), argues that modern histories have marginalised her largely because she was a woman.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Countess Amalie Elisabeth of Hanau-Münzenberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Landgravine Amalie Elisabeth</b> held the land together in the Thirty Years' War.</blockquote><a href="/state/landgraviate-of-hesse-kassel">Landgraviate of Hesse-Kassel</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hanau, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.13,8.92&amp;year=1633" aria-label="Hanau on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4754 420 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Amalie Elisabeth of Hanau-Münzenberg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5204 690h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5204 690h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.13,8.92&amp;year=1633">What was here in 1633? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
